1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,859
TRUDIE: OK, Stewart.

2
00:00:01,859 --> 00:00:06,040
We need to start planning our paper on public libraries

3
00:00:06,540 --> 00:00:08,180
Have you thought of an angle yet?

4
00:00:08,180 --> 00:00:11,140
STEWART: Well, there's so much we could look into

5
00:00:11,520 --> 00:00:14,315
How libraries have changed over the centuries,

6
00:00:14,315 --> 00:00:19,300
for instance, or how different countries organise them. What do you think, Trudie?

7
00:00:19,300 --> 00:00:21,985
TRUDIE: Maybe we should concentrate on this country,

8
00:00:21,985 --> 00:00:25,676
and try and relate the changes in libraries to external developments,

9
00:00:25,676 --> 00:00:30,039
like the fact that far more people can read than a century ago,

10
00:00:30,039 --> 00:00:33,730
and that the local population may speak lots of different languages.

11
00:00:33,730 --> 00:00:38,040
STEWART: We could include something about changes in the source of funding, too.

12
00:00:38,040 --> 00:00:41,746
TRUDIE: Yes, but remember we're only supposed to write a short paper,

13
00:00:41,746 --> 00:00:45,760
so it's probably best if we don't go into funding in any detail.

14
00:00:45,760 --> 00:00:51,750
STEWART: Right. Well, shall we just brainstorm a few ideas, to get started?

15
00:00:51,750 --> 00:00:57,115
TRUDIE: OK. We obviously need to look at the impact of new technology, particularly the internet.

16
00:00:57,115 --> 00:01:00,200
Now that lots of books have been digitalised

17
00:01:00,260 --> 00:01:03,150
people can access them from their own computers at home.

18
00:01:03,150 --> 00:01:06,830
STEWART: And if everyone did that, libraries would be obsolete.

19
00:01:06,830 --> 00:01:08,430
TRUDIE: Yes.

20
00:01:08,430 --> 00:01:15,814
STEWART: But the digitalised books that are available online for free are mostly out of copyright, aren't they?

21
00:01:15,814 --> 00:01:21,148
And copyright in this country lasts for 70 years after the author dies.

22
00:01:21,148 --> 00:01:25,250
So you won't find the latest best-seller or up-to-date information.

23
00:01:25,250 --> 00:01:26,842
TRUDIE: That's an important point.

24
00:01:26,842 --> 00:01:31,936
Anyway, I find it hard to concentrate when I'm reading a long text on a screen.

25
00:01:31,936 --> 00:01:37,030
I'd much rather read a physical book. And it takes longer to read on a screen.

26
00:01:37,030 --> 00:01:42,190
STEWART: Oh, I prefer it. I suppose it's just a personal preference.

27
00:01:42,190 --> 00:01:47,428
TRUDIE: Mm. I expect that libraries will go on evolving in the next few years.

28
00:01:47,428 --> 00:01:50,220
Some have already become centres where community activities take place

29
00:01:50,540 --> 00:01:52,666
like local clubs meeting there.

30
00:01:52,666 --> 00:01:55,110
I think that'll become even more common.

31
00:01:55,110 --> 00:01:57,104
STEWART: I'd like to think so,

32
00:01:57,104 --> 00:02:01,755
and that they'll still be serving their traditional function, but I'm not so sure.

33
00:02:01,755 --> 00:02:03,748
There are financial implications, after all.

34
00:02:03,748 --> 00:02:08,068
What I'm afraid will happen is that books and magazines will all disappear,

35
00:02:08,068 --> 00:02:11,058
and there'll just be rows and rows of computers.

36
00:02:11,058 --> 00:02:14,380
They won't look anything like the libraries we're used to.

37
00:02:14,380 --> 00:02:16,460
TRUDIE: Well, we'll see.

38
00:02:16,460 --> 00:02:18,385
TRUDIE: I've just had an idea.

39
00:02:18,385 --> 00:02:23,840
Why don't we make an in-depth study of our local public library as background to our paper?

40
00:02:23,840 --> 00:02:27,480
STEWART: Yes, that'd be interesting, and raise all sorts of issues.

41
00:02:27,480 --> 00:02:31,920
Let's make a list of possible things we could ask about

42
00:02:31,920 --> 00:02:33,435
then work out some sort of structure.

43
00:02:33,435 --> 00:02:36,744
For instance, um, we could interview some of the staff,

44
00:02:36,744 --> 00:02:39,940
and find out whether the library has its own budget

45
00:02:39,940 --> 00:02:42,700
or if that's controlled by the local council.

46
00:02:42,700 --> 00:02:44,395
TRUDIE: And what their policies are.

47
00:02:44,395 --> 00:02:46,480
I know they don't allow food

48
00:02:46,500 --> 00:02:49,481
but I'd love to find out what types of noise they ban -

49
00:02:49,481 --> 00:02:52,872
there always seems to be a lot of talking, but never music.

50
00:02:52,872 --> 00:02:55,980
I don't know if that's a policy or it just happens.

51
00:02:55,980 --> 00:03:03,281
STEWART: Ah, I've often wondered. Then there are things like how the library is affected by employment laws.

52
00:03:03,281 --> 00:03:08,960
I suppose there are rules about working hours, facilities for staff, and so on.

53
00:03:08,960 --> 00:03:13,400
TRUDIE: Right. Then there are other issues relating to the design of the building

54
00:03:13,400 --> 00:03:15,378
and how customers use it.

55
00:03:15,378 --> 00:03:19,094
Like what measures does the library take to ensure their safety?

56
00:03:19,094 --> 00:03:21,020
They'd need floor coverings that aren't slippery

57
00:03:21,520 --> 00:03:24,499
and emergency exits, for instance. Oh, and another thing -

58
00:03:24,499 --> 00:03:28,760
there's the question of the kind of insurance the library needs to have

59
00:03:28,760 --> 00:03:30,580
in case anyone gets injured.

60
00:03:30,580 --> 00:03:32,900
STEWART: Yes, that's something else to find out

61
00:03:33,680 --> 00:03:35,680
You know something I've often wondered?

62
00:03:35,680 --> 00:03:37,250
TRUDIE: What's that?

63
00:03:37,250 --> 00:03:41,928
STEWART: Well, you know they've got an archive of local newspapers going back years?

64
00:03:41,928 --> 00:03:47,608
Well, next to it they've got the diary of a well-known politician from the late 19th century.

65
00:03:47,608 --> 00:03:52,620
I wonder why it's there. Do you know what his connection was with this area?

66
00:03:52,620 --> 00:03:57,762
TRUDIE: No idea. Let's add it to our list of things to find out. Oh,

67
00:03:57,762 --> 00:04:03,590
I've just thought - you know people might ask in the library about local organisations, like sports clubs?

68
00:04:03,590 --> 00:04:08,390
Well. I wonder if they keep a database, or whether they just look online.

69
00:04:08,390 --> 00:04:14,539
STEWART: Right. I quite fancy finding out what the differences are between a library that's open to the public

70
00:04:14,539 --> 00:04:19,070
and one that's part of a museum, for example - they must be very different.

71
00:04:19,070 --> 00:04:22,080
TRUDIE: Mmm. Then something else I'd like to know is...
